BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2535@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T152000
SUMMARY:Thermal Framework Corner Cases: Bugs or Undefined Behavior?
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Daniel Lezcano · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2535/\n\nThe Linux thermal framewo
 rk has proven to be flexible and robust over the years. However\, some asp
 ects of its event handling have become increasingly difficult to reason ab
 out\, resulting in inconsistent behaviors and corner cases.\n\nExamples in
 clude trip point updates while the temperature has already fallen within t
 he hysteresis range after the notification was generated\, inconsistent ha
 ndling of thermal zones across system suspend and resume\, and other situa
 tions where the expected behavior is not clearly defined or documented.\n\
 nSome of these issues have been reported over the years\, while others hav
 e surfaced as new users and platforms have adopted the framework. Addressi
 ng them individually without a common understanding of the expected semant
 ics risks introducing further inconsistencies.\n\nThe goal of this discuss
 ion is to identify the current limitations of the thermal framework\, esta
 blish which behaviors are considered bugs versus intentional design choice
 s\, prioritize the issues that should be addressed\, and agree on a roadma
 p for improving the framework while preserving compatibility with existing
  users.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2535/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2539@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T152000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154000
SUMMARY:Power Cooling Devices: Bridging Thermal and Powercap
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Daniel Lezcano · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2539/\n\nThe Linux thermal managem
 ent and power capping frameworks currently evolve independently\, despite 
 relying on the same Energy Model (EM) to describe the power-performance ch
 aracteristics of devices.\n\nThe thermal framework regulates temperature b
 y estimating a sustainable power budget using the power_allocator governor
 . A PID control loop computes the power reduction required to maintain a t
 arget temperature\, and the Energy Model translates this budget into Opera
 ting Performance Point (OPP) constraints applied through thermal cooling d
 evices.\n\nThe powercap framework\, and in particular the Dynamic Thermal 
 Power Management (DTPM) controller\, also relies on the Energy Model to en
 force power budgets. However\, instead of manipulating OPPs directly\, it 
 distributes power limits across a hierarchy of power domains.\n\nAlthough 
 both frameworks address closely related problems and use the same underlyi
 ng model\, there is currently no mechanism to connect them. This raises th
 e question of whether a common abstraction could allow the thermal framewo
 rk to express power constraints through the powercap hierarchy rather than
  through device-specific OPP constraints.\n\nOne possible direction is the
  introduction of a power cooling device\, acting as a bridge between the t
 hermal and powercap frameworks. Such an abstraction could enable thermal g
 overnors to allocate power budgets to powercap domains while leaving power
  distribution and enforcement to the powercap subsystem. This approach may
  also open the door to hierarchical power budgeting and more coordinated t
 hermal management across heterogeneous devices.\n\nThe goal of this discus
 sion is to explore whether this direction makes sense from an architectura
 l perspective\, identify the challenges involved\, and agree on a set of i
 ncremental milestones that would allow such an integration to be developed
  and upstreamed progressively.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2539/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2536@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T160000
SUMMARY:Introducing Hardware Thermal Governor
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Srinivas Pandruvada 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2536/\n\nModern System-on-Chi
 p designs increasingly incorporate built-in hardware thermal controllers t
 o manage thermal conditions efficiently. Intel platforms\, beginning with 
 the Lunar Lake generation\, feature integrated platform temperature contro
 llers capable of autonomous thermal management. However\, not all platform
  designs directly interface temperature readings and thermal thresholds wi
 th these hardware governors\, limiting their effectiveness.\n\nThis presen
 tation introduces a kernel-level approach that leverages existing thermal 
 zone infrastructure to provide temperature data and threshold information 
 directly through a new hardware thermal governor\, bypassing user space in
 teractions.\n\nA proof-of-concept implementation will be demonstrated\, sh
 owcasing the integration of thermal zones with hardware-based thermal cont
 rol mechanisms.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2536/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2544@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T162000
SUMMARY:Devfreq for uncore DVFS
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Jie Zhan · https://
 lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2544/\n\nSoC uncore components\, such as
  interconnects\, system-level caches\, and memory controllers\, can accoun
 t for a substantial share of package power\, and their operating frequency
  bounds the achievable bandwidth and latency. Core DVFS is well supported 
 by cpufreq\, but there is no generic upstream mechanism for uncore DVFS - 
 a gap felt most on server platforms. We propose building uncore DVFS on to
 p of devfreq\, the DVFS framework for non-CPU devices. The device model fi
 ts\, but driving it from real utilization data exposes challenges worth di
 scussing.\n\nThis topic intends to cover:\n1. Motivation - why we need ker
 nel-side uncore DVFS.\n2. Existing upstream techniques - devfreq and vendo
 r drivers.\n3. Issues encountered - no way for a driver to obtain uncore P
 MU IDs and create perf events\; no suitable events or scaling policy for u
 ncore.\n4. Proposals and analysis - a perf core helper\, an event monitori
 ng and scaling policy\, and prototype measurements.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2544/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2537@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T164500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T170500
SUMMARY:Autonomous CPU performance selection: a new cpufreq governor?
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Sumit Gupta · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2537/\n\nACPI CPPC "autonomous select
 ion" lets the platform pick the CPU performance level itself\, within OS-p
 rovided min/max bounds and guided by an Energy Performance Preference (EPP
 ) hint. Today cppc_cpufreq exposes this as a per-policy auto_select sysfs 
 toggle layered on top of whatever scaling governor is attached. The result
  is confusing: once autonomous mode is on\, the attached governor (schedut
 il\, ondemand\, ...) keeps issuing frequency requests that the hardware ig
 nores\, and scaling_governor no longer reflects what actually drives frequ
 ency.\n\ncpufreq provides two ways to drive frequency\, and CPPC autonomou
 s mode fits neither. Scaling governors use the driver's ->target() callbac
 k to apply OS-selected operating points. setpolicy drivers (intel_pstate a
 nd amd-pstate "active"/EPP modes) instead let the hardware choose\, but CP
 PC has requirements it does not cover. With setpolicy\, the mode is select
 ed globally for all CPUs at the driver level\, whereas CPPC's registers ar
 e per-CPU and can be controlled per policy. setpolicy also exposes only th
 e performance and powersave policies and provides no ->target() path\, whi
 le the CPPC spec allows an OS desired_perf hint even when autonomous selec
 tion is enabled. So CPPC maps cleanly onto neither model.\n\nThis session 
 proposes a third option: modelling autonomous\, EPP-biased operation as a 
 per-policy cpufreq governor. Selecting the governor enables autonomous mod
 e and programs the EPP and min/max bounds\; switching away restores OS fre
 quency control. EPP is exposed as a per-policy governor tunable\, and the 
 autonomous state is reported read-only.\n\nThe broader question for the mi
 cro-conference is how cpufreq should represent hardware-autonomous selecti
 on in general:\n\n- Is a new cpufreq governor the right way to expose auto
 nomous selection at all?\n- If a governor is the right model\, should it b
 e CPPC-specific or generalized so other drivers can share it?\n- How shoul
 d it relate to the existing setpolicy model used by intel_pstate and amd-p
 state?\n- Should such a governor also pass utilization-based desired_perf 
 hints to the hardware?\n- How should the EPP control be exposed to userspa
 ce (naming\, accepted values\, and consistency with the existing energy_pe
 rformance_preference interface)?\n- What is the migration path away from t
 he current sysfs toggle without breaking existing users?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2537/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2579@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T170500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T172500
SUMMARY:Amortizing CPU wakeup costs
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Samuel Wu · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2579/\n\nTransitioning a CPU into and o
 ut of idle has a non-negligible energy overhead. During low-utilization st
 ates\, frequent background wakeups repeatedly pay this "wakeup tax." Becau
 se the Energy Model only prices active OPP power and not idle-state transi
 tion costs\, EAS defaults to spreading tasks across a cluster rather than 
 packing them.\n\nPrototyping with sched_ext on a Pixel 9 running Linux 7.1
  as a proof-of-concept\, we measure the power and latency tradeoffs of bat
 ching non-urgent wakeups onto already-active CPUs. We show that reducing i
 dle exits measurably lowers CPU power\, and that the tail-latency penalty 
 of packing comes from committing tasks to a specific CPU at wakeup time. D
 eferring CPU selection until dispatch time achieves the same power savings
  at a fraction of the latency cost.\n\nDiscussion points:\n\n- Energy Awar
 e Scheduling: Should the Energy Model expose an idle-exit cost term\, or r
 ely on a low-utilization packing heuristic?\n- Cluster-scoped deferral: Ca
 n we support cluster-scoped task push-pull (similar to RT pushable tasks) 
 instead of committing at select_task_rq()? Would this make it easier to ba
 tch?\n- Classifying latency-tolerance: What task QoS hints and system-stat
 e signals should govern wakeup deferral?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2579/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2543@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T172500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T174500
SUMMARY:Current developments in the Common Clk Framework
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Brian Masney · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2543/\n\nAt last year's Linux Plumbe
 rs Conference\, we had some great discussions about how to fix clock tree 
 propagation in the Common Clk Framework (https://lpc.events/event/19/contr
 ibutions/2152/). Taking that feedback into account\, a v8 patch set has be
 en posted that solves the problem in a simple manner.\n\nhttps://lore.kern
 el.org/linux-clk/20260327-clk-scaling-v8-0-86cd0aba3c5f@redhat.com/\n\nThe
  KUnit tests demonstrate the problem where a clock can unknowingly change 
 the rate of its parent and siblings to suboptimal frequencies. This can ha
 ve adverse effects on subsystems that need precise rates\, such as DRM and
  sound. The patch set needs more eyes from the community to get across the
  finish line. We’d like to use part of this session to go over the appro
 ach and figure out how to get it merged.\n\nWe can use the remainder of th
 e time to discuss suggestions for ways to replace the clk subsystem's glob
 al prepare lock with a more fine-grained locking mechanism.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2543/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2580@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T175000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T181000
SUMMARY:Hardware Pressure on x86: Does the Linux Scheduler Need to Know Whe
 n You're Throttled?
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Ricardo Neri · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2580/\n\nThe scheduler's task wake-u
 p logic and capacity-aware load balancing rely on arch_scale_cpu_capacity(
 ) to determine how much work a CPU can absorb. It also has logic to accoun
 t for the effects of transient thermal- and power-driven capacity loss. Th
 is mechanism\, known as hardware pressure\, is currently not used on x86: 
 arch_scale_cpu_capacity() returns a fixed constant on non-hybrid systems\,
  and even on hybrid systems it is a self-normalizing ratio that hides *uni
 form\, proportional* capacity loss across all cores\, since the reference 
 CPU used for normalization throttles along with the rest. No plumbing exis
 ts to feed x86 thermal/power status signals (PROCHOT\, RAPL power-limitati
 on bits\, HWP capability changes) into capacity accounting.\n\nThis gap ha
 s consequences. Task placement at wake-up and EAS's\noverutilized-detectio
 n gate can silently keep placing or retaining tasks on a throttled CPU\, b
 ecause the capacity term used for comparison never reflects the throttling
 \; the load balancer may likewise\nmove tasks onto throttled CPUs. This is
  not a purely theoretical\nconcern: single-core thermal throttling is comm
 on even on non-hybrid parts\, and Intel Speed Select Technology’s Core P
 ower makes persistent\, policy-driven asymmetric power allocation across c
 ores.\n\nThis talk proposes a design for exposing hardware pressure on x86
  and demonstrates it against concrete use cases on real hardware. The goal
  is not to present a finished solution\, but to solicit feedback on whethe
 r this problem is worth solving\, and if so\, whether the proposed solutio
 n makes sense.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2580/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2595@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T181000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T183000
SUMMARY:Dynamic Runtime Prediction of CPU Idle-State Exit Latency
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Power Management and Thermal Control MC · Aiqun Yu\, Cong Zhan
 g · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2595/\n\nBenefits of Accura
 te Exit Latency can have:\n\n**More Accurate hrtimer Expiration**\n**Bette
 r CPU Idle level Selection**\n**Improved Support for Latency-Sensitive Sys
 tems**\n\nCpu different low power state can have different exit latency. A
 nd the exit latency may be affected by:\n\n 1. current cpu frequency\n 2. 
 Different firmware version\n 3. Different hardware difference and etc.\n\n
 So compile time static idle exit latency is not sufficient. Hence propose 
 to have dynamic Runtime Prediction of CPU Idle-State Exit Latency\n\n**Mor
 e Accurate hrtimer Expiration**\nDue to exit latency\, the actual executio
 n time of a timer interrupt is often later than the programmed expiration 
 time. With an accurate estimate of exit latency\, the timer expiration can
  be advanced accordingly\, improving timer firing precision.\n\n    QQ Mus
 ic\n    Version	Max Delay (ns)	Average Delay (ns)\n    Original	2\,446\,25
 4	618\,558\n    Original	2\,859\,657	664\,312\n    Original	1\,916\,403	46
 6\,087\n    Optimized	629\,050	86\,448\n    Optimized	674\,887	107\,095\n 
    Optimized	868\,674	102\,682\n    Honor of Kings (30 Hz)\n    Version	Ma
 x Delay (ns)	Average Delay (ns)\n    Original	2\,309\,583	14\,470\n    Opt
 imized	360\,256	8\,234\n\nNote that data is collected from an old kernel v
 ersion and legacy qcom platform.\n\n**Better CPU Idle-State Selection**\nT
 he current TOE cpuidle governor estimates idle duration using Exit Latency
  / 2. A more accurate exit latency estimation allows the governor to deriv
 e an idle duration closer to the actual value\, leading to more appropriat
 e idle-state selection.\n\nImproved Support for Latency-Sensitive Systems\
 nAccurate exit latency prediction is particularly beneficial for latency-s
 ensitive workloads\, such as:\n	• PREEMPT_RT systems\n	• Real-time app
 lications\n	• Interactive workloads like audio scenario\n\nObtaining Acc
 urate Exit Latency at Runtime (Monitor)\n	• Exit latency is fundamentall
 y defined as:\nExit Latency = System Resume Timestamp − Wakeup Event Tim
 estamp\n	• The current cpuidle governor already compares predicted idle 
 duration against actual idle duration. However\, the existing measurement 
 only covers the interval between the last instruction before entering idle
  and the first instruction after wakeup. Exit latency itself is typically 
 estimated using a static value defined in the device tree.\n	• In practi
 ce\, exit latency varies dynamically depending on runtime conditions and s
 ystem state.\n	• There are many possible wakeup sources. To accurately d
 etermine the wakeup-event timestamp\, measurements are restricted to timer
 -based wakeups because the timer expiration time is known.\n	• To ensure
  measurement accuracy\, only idle exits triggered exclusively by timer int
 errupts should be considered\, i.e.\, the timer interrupt is the only pend
 ing interrupt when the CPU wakes up.\n	• CPU idle states are also dynami
 c. As additional CPUs enter idle\, the cluster-level idle state may change
 . Therefore\, the actual idle state must be determined dynamically both wh
 en entering and exiting idle\, ensuring that collected historical data is 
 correctly associated with the corresponding idle level.\n\nExit Latency Pr
 ediction (Predict)\n	• Exit latency is influenced by multiple factors an
 d continuously changes during runtime.\n	• A sliding-window-based approa
 ch can be used to collect multiple exit-latency samples.\n	• Outliers ar
 e filtered out\, and the average of the remaining samples is used as the p
 redicted exit latency.\n\nUtilizing Exit Latency (Control)\nMore Accurate 
 Idle-Time Estimation\nThe cpuidle governor can use the predicted exit late
 ncy to improve idle-duration estimation accuracy\, resulting in better idl
 e-state selection.\n\nImproved Timer Accuracy on Broadcast-Timer Platforms
 \nOn platforms supporting a broadcast timer\, multiple CPUs may need to wa
 ke up simultaneously. Since a broadcast timer interrupt can only be delive
 red to a single CPU initially\, additional wakeup delay is introduced for 
 the remaining CPUs.\nTo compensate for this effect\, the exit latencies of
  multiple CPUs can be accumulated and applied to the first awakened CPU\, 
 producing a more accurate timer expiration schedule.\n\nSummary of Benefit
 s\nAccurate exit latency prediction provides the following advantages:\n	
 • More accurate idle-time estimation for the cpuidle governor.\n	• Imp
 roved CPU idle-state selection.\n	• Higher timer firing accuracy through
  compensation for exit latency.\n	• Reduced wakeup latency on broadcast-
 timer platforms.\n	• Better performance for latency-sensitive systems su
 ch as PREEMPT_RT.\n\nOpen Discussions\n	• During evaluation\, a certain 
 percentage of outlier samples is still observed within the sampling window
 . Currently\, these samples are removed directly to reduce their impact on
  prediction accuracy.\n	• On platforms running multiple virtual machines
 \, the timer virtualization mechanism may affect the effectiveness and acc
 uracy of exit-latency prediction and compensation. Further investigation i
 s required.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2595/
END:VEVENT
